From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 24 May 2026 12:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] OpKernel registry: tolerate duplicate kernel registrations

In the systemlib build the same kernel-registration TU is embedded in both
libtensorflow_framework.so and libtensorflow_cc.so (both loaded at import),
so identical kernels such as _Send/_Recv register twice in the process-global
registry. On a GPU host the first host<->device copy then aborts with
"Multiple OpKernel registrations match NodeDef at the same priority"
(GPU-less CI never exercises this). Skip an exact-duplicate registration
(same key, kernel_class_name and serialized KernelDef) in
OpKernelRegistrar::InitInternal; genuinely distinct kernels are still added.
Mirrors the OpRegistry/PlatformObjectRegistry duplicate-tolerance patches.
---
--- a/tensorflow/core/framework/op_kernel.cc
+++ b/tensorflow/core/framework/op_kernel.cc
@@ -1359,6 +1359,21 @@
   auto global_registry =
       reinterpret_cast<KernelRegistry*>(GlobalKernelRegistry());
   mutex_lock l(global_registry->mu);
+  // conda-forge: in the systemlib build the same kernel-registration TU is
+  // embedded in more than one loaded TF .so (libtensorflow_framework.so and
+  // libtensorflow_cc.so), so identical kernels (e.g. _Send/_Recv) register
+  // twice in this process-global registry and FindKernelRegistration then
+  // aborts on a GPU host with "Multiple OpKernel registrations match ... at
+  // the same priority". Skip an exact duplicate (same key, class name and
+  // KernelDef); genuinely distinct kernels for the op are still added.
+  auto existing = global_registry->registry.equal_range(key);
+  for (auto it = existing.first; it != existing.second; ++it) {
+    if (it->second.kernel_class_name == kernel_class_name &&
+        it->second.def.SerializeAsString() == kernel_def->SerializeAsString()) {
+      delete kernel_def;
+      return;
+    }
+  }
   global_registry->registry.emplace(
       key,
       KernelRegistration(*kernel_def, kernel_class_name, std::move(factory)));
